{"person_id": "masatoshi-shima", "name": "Masatoshi Shima", "category": "Microprocessor logic designer (Intel 4004, Z80)", "birth_year": 1943, "age_at_milestone": 25, "milestone_by_age_26": "At Busicom (from 1967), designed special-purpose LSI calculator logic (1968, age 25) and in 1969 (age 25-26) worked with Intel's Ted Hoff and Stanley Mazor to reduce the design into the one-chip CPU architecture that became the Intel 4004.", "early_history_summary": "Born August 22, 1943 in Shizuoka, Japan. Studied organic chemistry at Tohoku University (B.S. 1967); poor chemistry job prospects led him to Busicom calculators where he learned software and digital logic (1967-68). Led Busicom's multi-chip calculator logic proposal and collaborated with Intel on the simplified 4004 architecture before assisting Federico Faggin on silicon implementation in 1970.", "primary_leverage_engine": "Scarce technical / intellectual depth", "normalized_primary_engine": "Scarce technical / intellectual depth", "secondary_engine": "Product / domain insight", "started_serious_reps_before_20_score": 0, "prior_reps_score": 2, "scarce_skill_depth_score": 3, "native_distribution_score": 0, "elite_ecosystem_network_score": 1, "complementary_team_score": 2, "structural_wave_score": 2, "concentration_intensity_score": 3, "capital_safety_score": 0, "domain_proximity_score": 2, "success_tier": 2, "leverage_evidence_confidence": "High", "family_context_summary": "Born Shizuoka, Japan, 1943; detailed family financial/professional background not documented in reviewed English sources.", "parent_family_domain_summary": "Not documented in reviewed sources.", "early_family_financial_platform_support_score": 0, "parent_family_domain_advantage_score": 0, "inherited_audience_business_network_score": 0, "elite_institution_performance_pipeline_score": 1, "frontier_geography_ecosystem_score": 0, "rare_early_tools_facilities_score": 0, "dedicated_mentor_coach_tutor_score": 0, "exceptional_peer_cofounder_sibling_score